   "text": "## Summary\n\nThis is a large, legacy-style OpenEMR form report file. It has several real security problems (SQL injection via string concatenation, inconsistent/missing output escaping leading to XSS, no authorization check), at least one functional bug (a `$choice` request-key mismatch), and a lot of maintainability debt (variable-variable extraction from DB rows, large blocks of dead/commented code, inconsistent globals usage). Details below.\n\n---\n\n### 1. SQL injection via string concatenation\n**Location:** `display_draw_image()`\n```php\n$base_name = $pid.\"_\".$encounter.\"_\".$side.\"_\".$zone.\"_VIEW\";\n$filename = $base_name.\".jpg\";\n$sql = \"SELECT * from documents where documents.url like '%\".$filename.\"'\";\n$doc = sqlQuery($sql);\n```\n`$pid` and `$encounter` originate from `$_REQUEST['ptid']`/`$_REQUEST['encid']` and are concatenated directly into a query instead of using a parameterized placeholder (as is correctly done elsewhere in this same file, e.g. `sqlQuery($query, array($encounter, $pid))`). Relying on `$sanitize_all_escapes` legacy global escaping instead of prepared statements is fragile and inconsistent.\n**Severity:** High (security)\n\nSimilar concatenated-SQL patterns exist for the facility lookup:\n```php\n$sql = \"select * from facility where id=\" . $_SESSION['pc_facility'];\n```\nLess risky since it's session-derived, but still should use a bound parameter.\n**Severity:** Low/Medium\n\n---\n\n### 2. Inconsistent/missing output escaping \u2192 stored XSS\n**Location:** `narrative()`\n```php\n&nbsp;<?php echo $HPI1; ?>              // not escaped (HPI2/HPI3 correctly use text())\n...\necho $item['codetext'].\"<br />\";        // IMPPLAN loop, not escaped\necho $item['code'].\"<br />\";            // not escaped\necho  $item['plan'].\"</div><br />\";     // not escaped\n...\n$PLAN_items = explode('|',$PLAN);\nforeach ($PLAN_items as $item) { echo  $item.\"<br />\"; }\nif ($PLAN2) { echo $PLAN2.\"<br />\"; }\n```\nThese values come from free-text clinical fields and are echoed verbatim without `text()`/`htmlspecialchars()`, while nearly identical neighboring fields *are* escaped. This is a classic stored-XSS vector, especially since this report may be rendered in a shared encounter-history view accessible to other staff.\n**Severity:** High (security)\n\n---\n\n### 3. No authorization/ACL check for patient data access\n**Location:** top of file / `require_once` block\nThe file reads `ptid`, `encid`, `formid` straight from `$_REQUEST` and uses them to query and display a patient's clinical record, but there is no `acl_check()`/ownership check verifying the logged-in user is permitted to view that patient's data. `library/acl.inc` is included but never invoked. Combined with issue #1, this is also an IDOR risk \u2014 any authenticated user can view any patient encounter by guessing/incrementing `ptid`/`encid`.\n**Severity:** High (security)\n\n---\n\n### 4. Logic bug: mismatched request keys for `choice`\n**Location:**\n```php\nif ($_REQUEST['CHOICE']) $choice = $_REQUEST['choice'];\n```\nChecks `CHOICE` (uppercase) but reads `choice` (lowercase). Since the docblock explicitly says callers pass a lowercase `choice` parameter, this condition will typically be false and `$choice` will never be set from the request, defeating the TEXT/DRAW/narrative branching this file is built around.\n**Severity:** Medium (correctness)\n\n---\n\n### 5. Unbounded `extract()` from DB rows / dynamic variable-variables\n**Location:** multiple (`eye_mag_report()`, `narrative()`, prefs loop, wearing-glasses loop)\n```php\n@extract($objQuery);\n...\n$LOCATION = $prefs['LOCATION'];\n$$LOCATION = text($prefs['GOVALUE']);\n...\n${\"ODSPH_$count_rx\"} = $wearing['ODSPH'];\n```\nDumping arbitrary DB columns into the local/global symbol table (with `@` suppressing notices) makes the code very hard to reason about and can silently clobber important variables (`$pid`, `$choice`, `$form_folder`, etc.) if a column name ever collides. This is a known OpenEMR antipattern but should be flagged as a maintainability/robustness risk, not continued in new code.\n**Severity:** Medium (maintainability/reliability)\n\n---\n\n### 6. Dead / commented-out code left in the file\n**Location:** `eye_mag_report()` DRAW branch (large `/* ... */` block with a `width=100 heght=75` typo), `left_overs()`, `full_report()` (immediate `return;` making the rest unreachable)\nThese add significant noise, are not exercised, and `left_overs()` references undeclared globals (`$table_name`, `$id`) that would break if ever called.\n**Severity:** Low (maintainability)\n\n---\n\n### 7. Inconsistent/undefined globals\n**Location:** `narrative()`, `display_draw_image()`\n- `$GLOBALS['webroot']` is used in several places while `$GLOBALS['web_root']` is used elsewhere for the same purpose \u2014 likely a typo that will silently produce broken image URLs if `webroot` isn't actually set.\n- `$css_header` is echoed (`attr($css_header)`) but never defined/set in this file; relies implicitly on caller's global state.\n**Severity:** Low/Medium (correctness, hard to detect without testing)\n\n---\n\n### 8. No input type casting on identifiers\n**Location:** top-level `$_REQUEST` handling\n```php\nif ($_REQUEST['ptid']) $pid = $_REQUEST['ptid'];\nif ($_REQUEST['encid']) $encounter=$_REQUEST['encid'];\nif ($_REQUEST['formid']) $form_id = $_REQUEST['formid'];\n```\nThese should be cast to `(int)` given they are used as numeric IDs in queries and file-name construction; this is cheap defense-in-depth against #1 and path-based issues in the (currently dead) DRAW block.\n**Severity:** Low/Medium\n\n---\n\n### Minor nits\n- File has no trailing newline / closing `?>` left in a `.php` file (stray whitespace after can cause \"headers already sent\" issues if this file is ever `include`d rather than used purely as a function library).\n- `$formname` parameter of `eye_mag_report()` is accepted but never used.\n- `report_ACT()` uses `ENT_NOQUOTES`, weaker than `ENT_QUOTES`, when used purely for HTML body content; low risk but worth tightening for consistency with the rest of OpenEMR's `text()` helper semantics."
